// The parts exercise 4 asks you to add, brought by the check itself.
//
// WHY THE CHECK BRINGS THEM
//
// You add the sensor and the four LEDs in the board view, which writes them
// into your own board.repl. That file stays on your machine and in your
// repository; the grader takes only exercises/*/main.c from a submission, so
// your board.repl never reaches it. A check that waited for your parts would
// therefore never be able to look at anything on the grading side.
//
// So the check declares them here, at the pins and the address the exercise
// fixes, and loads this on top of boards/nucleo_f401re.repl. The same thing
// verify/sensor.repl does for the setup self-test, and for the same reason.
//
// This is NOT loaded when you run the board yourself. Your board is still
// board.repl, and the parts on it are still the ones you put there.
//
// WHAT THIS MEANS FOR WHAT IS CHECKED
//
// Your firmware is checked; your wiring is not. Renode's I2C model never
// reads the GPIO registers, so the clock enables, the alternate-function
// mode and the open-drain output type cannot be verified here at all --
// see 'pin-configuration-not-enforced' and 'open-drain-ignored' in
// DIVERGENCES.md. Do the pin setup properly anyway: on a real board it is
// the difference between working and not.

si7021: Sensors.SI70xx @ i2c1 0x40
    model: Model.SI7021

led_d2: Miscellaneous.LED @ gpioPortA
led_d3: Miscellaneous.LED @ gpioPortB
led_d4: Miscellaneous.LED @ gpioPortB
led_d5: Miscellaneous.LED @ gpioPortB

gpioPortA:
    10 -> led_d2@0

gpioPortB:
    3 -> led_d3@0
    5 -> led_d4@0
    4 -> led_d5@0
